WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.171 --> 00:00:12.055
<v Maya>Welcome to The Deep Dive. Today we are opening the black box of artificial intelligence in the workplace. Because, you know, every company on Earth has bought Gen-AI tools for their employees by now.</v>

2
00:00:12.255 --> 00:00:13.335
<v Daniel>Oh, yeah. Everyone.</v>

3
00:00:13.576 --> 00:00:17.037
<v Maya>But almost nobody actually knows if their employees are any good at using them.</v>

4
00:00:17.277 --> 00:00:17.457
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

5
00:00:17.977 --> 00:00:32.852
<v Maya>So our mission today is to answer that exact question. We're going to dig into a brand new, highly anticipated academic working paper to find out who actually knows what they're doing with these tools and why the current</v>

6
00:00:32.892 --> 00:00:35.955
<v Maya>approach to corporate training might just be completely broken.</v>

7
00:00:36.015 --> 00:00:37.757
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's a fascinating look under the hood.</v>

8
00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:49.448
<v Maya>But before we get to the data, I want you to think about the corporate AI rollout kind of like this. It's a bit like a company buying everyone on staff a top of the line professional grade espresso machine for their desks.</v>

9
00:00:49.708 --> 00:00:52.931
<v Daniel>Which, I mean, sounds like a very generous, highly caffeinated experiment.</v>

10
00:00:52.951 --> 00:01:01.579
<v Maya>Right. It does. And the procurement team checks the box. They look at their dashboard and they say, great, espresso machines deployed. We have 100 percent adoption.</v>

11
00:01:01.679 --> 00:01:02.440
<v Daniel>Mission accomplished.</v>

12
00:01:02.828 --> 00:01:17.555
<v Maya>Exactly. But if you walk the floor and look at what people are actually doing, most people are just using this incredibly complex machine to dispense hot water for their tea. Yeah. They're using maybe 10% of its capability because no one actually taught them how to, you know, pull a shot,</v>

13
00:01:17.615 --> 00:01:29.240
<v Maya>dial in the pressure, steam the milk. Adoption is incredibly easy to measure. But skill, figuring out if anyone actually knows how to pull a good espresso, that has been a total blind spot.</v>

14
00:01:29.484 --> 00:01:44.372
<v Daniel>It really has. It is the defining blind spot of this whole workplace technology wave. And that is exactly why this new working paper is so important. Yeah. It was just drafted recently, August 25, 2026. It's called Sophistication in Gen I</v>

15
00:01:44.512 --> 00:01:54.097
<v Daniel>Use, Field Evidence from a Large Firm. And it's by Nicholas Hallman, Zachary Kowalewski, and Yemi Schmidt from the McComb School of Business, along with Anupu Vada from KPMG.</v>

16
00:01:54.237 --> 00:02:03.979
<v Maya>And we should just state really clearly right up front, this is a working caper. It's not yet peer reviewed. It was written by accounting and business school researchers who were granted data access by KPMG.</v>

17
00:02:04.059 --> 00:02:04.239
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

18
00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:09.420
<v Maya>We aren't doing a deep dive on this because it's in some flashy journal. We're doing it because of the data.</v>

19
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.881
<v Daniel>Oh, totally. The data is genuinely rare.</v>

20
00:02:12.101 --> 00:02:12.321
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

21
00:02:12.461 --> 00:02:17.622
<v Daniel>Because until now, nearly every study of AI in the workplace has relied on one of three things.</v>

22
00:02:17.742 --> 00:02:18.842
<v Maya>Right. Like surveys.</v>

23
00:02:19.002 --> 00:02:31.828
<v Daniel>Exactly. Surveys, which just tell you what people think they do. Or randomized trials of access, which basically just tell you if giving someone the tool helped them. Or you get platform-wide data from the vendors themselves.</v>

24
00:02:31.948 --> 00:02:33.629
<v Maya>Right, which has its own biases.</v>

25
00:02:33.649 --> 00:02:46.377
<v Daniel>Yeah. But this paper, this paper has the actual raw chat transcripts from inside a single organization. And crucially, those transcripts are linked to each employee's seniority, their functional department, and their corporate training records.</v>

26
00:02:46.828 --> 00:02:48.292
<v Maya>Nobody else has had this combination.</v>

27
00:02:48.392 --> 00:02:48.793
<v Daniel>Nobody.</v>

28
00:02:48.853 --> 00:03:03.734
<v Maya>Okay, let's unpack this because the scale is massive. We were talking about 713,564 user prompts. Wow. Yeah, across over 158,000 conversations. This involves almost 4,000 back office employees</v>

29
00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:08.416
<v Maya>across 15 different functional areas from January through August of 2025. It's</v>

30
00:03:08.456 --> 00:03:09.337
<v Daniel>huge.</v>

31
00:03:09.857 --> 00:03:24.565
<v Maya>And they were using an internal tool called AIQ Chat, which routed them to commercial models like Anthropix, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, OpenAI's GPT-40 and 01, Google's Gemini, and Meta's Llama. So we finally have this</v>

32
00:03:24.785 --> 00:03:27.426
<v Maya>fly on the wall view of corporate AI use in the wild.</v>

33
00:03:27.806 --> 00:03:42.372
<v Daniel>And the very first thing this fly on the wall sees is that adoption is solved, but skill is absolutely not. Right. This paper really reframes the whole AI at work conversation by showing this massive disconnect. If we go back to your espresso machine</v>

34
00:03:42.412 --> 00:03:48.895
<v Daniel>analogy, the paper shows that 83.9% of employee months include the use of at least one AI tool.</v>

35
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:59.506
<v Maya>Which is astronomical when you compare it to the national average. I mean, the BIC, Blandin, and Deming survey from around the same time showed only about 32% of U.S. workers reported using Gen AI for work.</v>

36
00:03:59.526 --> 00:04:00.167
<v Daniel>Yeah.</v>

37
00:04:00.187 --> 00:04:05.550
<v Maya>So this firm is the absolute best case scenario. They bought it, they encouraged it, and they got near universal adoption.</v>

38
00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:11.914
<v Daniel>Right. But that broad adoption masks an enormous, wildly skewed variation in how intensely people use it.</v>

39
00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:13.115
<v Maya>Okay. How skewed are we talking?</v>

40
00:04:13.497 --> 00:04:19.742
<v Daniel>Well, the median active employee in this data set has just 14 conversations over an eight-month period.</v>

41
00:04:20.042 --> 00:04:21.484
<v Maya>Wow, that's practically nothing.</v>

42
00:04:21.684 --> 00:04:26.748
<v Daniel>Right. Meanwhile, the top 10% of users account for 51% of all conversations.</v>

43
00:04:28.083 --> 00:04:31.324
<v Maya>So half the usage is coming from a tiny fraction of the workforce.</v>

44
00:04:31.444 --> 00:04:32.004
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

45
00:04:32.024 --> 00:04:46.529
<v Maya>So if adoption is basically universal, but the actual usage is that concentrated, how do the researchers figure out who is actually good at it? Because, I mean, they obviously couldn't sit down and manually read 150,000 chat transcripts.</v>

46
00:04:46.589 --> 00:04:50.890
<v Daniel>No, no, they couldn't. So they had to use AI to measure the AI use. Oh, of</v>

47
00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:51.210
<v Maya>course.</v>

48
00:04:51.450 --> 00:05:05.540
<v Daniel>They define sophistication as skilled use, informed by an understanding of how the tool works and where it applies. So they fed every single transcript into an advanced large language model, OpenAI's O1, with a highly structured metaprompt.</v>

49
00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:06.060
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

50
00:05:06.521 --> 00:05:12.367
<v Daniel>And they essentially asked the model to score the human's prompts and aggregate them into three composite measures of sophistication.</v>

51
00:05:12.596 --> 00:05:24.965
<v Maya>Let's break those three measures down because I think a lot of listeners will want to know if they themselves count as sophisticated users. So the first bucket is prompt clarity. Like, are they organizing their thoughts? Are they being specific?</v>

52
00:05:25.246 --> 00:05:38.796
<v Daniel>Yes. And are they asking for structured output? For example, are they asking the AI to format the answer as a specific coding language like JSON or as a heavily formatted data table rather than just giving them a wall of text?</v>

53
00:05:39.225 --> 00:05:53.977
<v Maya>Got it. Okay. The second bucket is deliberate strategy use. And this is where we get into the heavy jargon of prompt engineering. They looked for things like role prompting, you know, saying act as a senior auditor. Right. But they also looked for few shot examples and chain of</v>

54
00:05:54.017 --> 00:05:58.240
<v Maya>thought. Wait, remind me really quickly so we don't lose anyone. What exactly are those two in practice?</v>

55
00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:06.587
<v Daniel>Absolutely. So few shot examples just means you don't just ask the AI to do a task. You provide it with two or three completed examples of what good looks like first.</v>

56
00:06:06.767 --> 00:06:08.108
<v Maya>Oh, so it can spot the pattern.</v>

57
00:06:08.228 --> 00:06:19.285
<v Daniel>Exactly. Chain of thought is when you explicitly ask the AI to show its work step by step before it gives you the final answer. It forces the model to reason through a problem, which dramatically reduces errors.</v>

58
00:06:19.570 --> 00:06:33.981
<v Maya>OK, that makes sense. And then the third bucket is use case diversity. So is this employee only using the AI as a glorified spell checker or are they applying it across a broad mix of tasks like analyzing data,</v>

59
00:06:34.322 --> 00:06:36.163
<v Maya>brainstorming, writing code?</v>

60
00:06:36.303 --> 00:06:40.046
<v Daniel>Yeah, exactly. Now, I do have to inject a very important, honest caveat here.</v>

61
00:06:40.126 --> 00:06:40.387
<v Maya>OK.</v>

62
00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:55.982
<v Daniel>The authors are extremely explicit about this. These three buckets are proxies for skill. They are measuring the quality and structure of the prompts themselves. Right. They are not measuring whether the employee actually got what they wanted or whether the</v>

63
00:06:56.042 --> 00:07:03.107
<v Daniel>final output quality improved. We are measuring how well they operate the steering wheel, not whether they arrived at the correct destination.</v>

64
00:07:03.127 --> 00:07:13.274
<v Maya>That's a great point. But looking at how they operate the steering wheel is fascinating because the texture of real everyday work is just so vivid in this data. And honestly, the numbers are a little deflating.</v>

65
00:07:13.294 --> 00:07:14.015
<v Daniel>Yeah, they really are.</v>

66
00:07:14.435 --> 00:07:29.329
<v Maya>Because writing dominates completely. Over 73% of conversations are just writing, mostly just editing text the user pasted in or generating new text. Knowledge queries are a distant second at 23%. Coding and data analysis all the way down</v>

67
00:07:29.529 --> 00:07:30.390
<v Maya>at 7%.</v>

68
00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:34.854
<v Daniel>Which perfectly aligns with the background research we have from platform-wide data.</v>

69
00:07:34.914 --> 00:07:35.555
<v Maya>Right, right.</v>

70
00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:50.721
<v Daniel>If you look at the Antropic Economic Index paper by Handa and colleagues, they mapped 4 million clawed conversations and found the exact same thing. Writing and software development dominate. The Handa paper shows us what the economy is doing with AI, but this Hallman paper shows us how</v>

71
00:07:50.781 --> 00:07:51.942
<v Daniel>well individuals are doing it.</v>

72
00:07:52.264 --> 00:08:06.571
<v Maya>And the answer to how well is, well, it's pretty rough. Explicit constraints, like telling the AI exactly how long the response should be or what tone to use, that happens in only about 24% of chats. Requests for structured output, 2.9%.</v>

73
00:08:07.112 --> 00:08:12.174
<v Maya>Giving the AI explicit acceptance criteria so it can check its own work, 1%. It's</v>

74
00:08:12.354 --> 00:08:12.895
<v Daniel>so low.</v>

75
00:08:13.235 --> 00:08:22.481
<v Maya>And those few shot examples and chain of thought requests we just talked about. Each of those appears in less than 1% of real workplace conversations.</v>

76
00:08:23.001 --> 00:08:31.828
<v Daniel>The gap between the theoretical prompt engineering literature and actual desk work is just staggering. I mean, think about the Schulhof prompt report from 2024.</v>

77
00:08:31.949 --> 00:08:33.510
<v Maya>Oh, yeah, the taxonomy.</v>

78
00:08:33.965 --> 00:08:48.137
<v Daniel>Yeah, that was a massive systematic survey that cataloged 58 distinct text-based prompting techniques. An entire research literature and a whole cottage industry of online courses was built around these 58 techniques.</v>

79
00:08:48.397 --> 00:09:02.946
<v Maya>And yet in the wild, any deliberate strategy at all only shows up 5.2% of the time. Exactly. But you know what does show up? Pleasantries. Yes. 30% of conversations include people saying please and thank you to the software. Nearly 11% of chats have frequent</v>

80
00:09:02.986 --> 00:09:05.987
<v Maya>typos. Over 5% are written entirely in lowercase letters.</v>

81
00:09:06.367 --> 00:09:12.949
<v Daniel>It paints a very clear, very human picture of how casually people are interacting with these incredibly powerful systems.</v>

82
00:09:13.497 --> 00:09:28.202
<v Maya>Here's where it gets really interesting, though, because I look at that and my first instinct is, wow, the workforce is completely failing to learn how to use this tool. Right. But I have to push back on that a bit. Is it a failure of the workforce or is it a sign that the models have gotten</v>

83
00:09:28.322 --> 00:09:34.084
<v Maya>so good that all this complex 58 technique scaffolding just isn't needed anymore?</v>

84
00:09:34.404 --> 00:09:49.210
<v Daniel>And that is the exact debate at the frontier of AI right now. We have to admit that this paper cannot fully settle it. Both interpretations are entirely valid. Maybe workers are leaving massive amounts of value on the table by not using those advanced techniques. Or maybe those techniques were just</v>

85
00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:56.973
<v Daniel>temporary crutches for weaker models back in 2023. And by 2025, the models just understand what you want, even if you type it in all lowercase with typos.</v>

86
00:09:57.402 --> 00:10:06.626
<v Maya>Either way, the researchers did find clear patterns in who uses the tool with more sophistication, and their first major finding totally contradicts the conventional wisdom.</v>

87
00:10:07.026 --> 00:10:17.190
<v Daniel>It really does. Because the standard narrative, the one every executive seems to believe, is that the digital native youth will naturally outperform their older, more senior colleagues with new tech.</v>

88
00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:32.639
<v Maya>But the data says the exact opposite. All three sophistication measures rise consistently step by step with seniority. Junior staff score the lowest, managers score higher, and above manager employees score the highest. Senior employees also use the</v>

89
00:10:32.679 --> 00:10:39.926
<v Maya>tools more often, and they use them for complex knowledge retrieval while junior staff skew heavily toward basic writing and personal requests.</v>

90
00:10:40.474 --> 00:10:54.661
<v Daniel>We should bring in the Humlum Investor Guard study from 2025 here, which surveyed 18,000 Danish workers. Humlum found that younger workers adopt AI at much higher rates, and that every additional year of age predicted a lower likelihood of using the tool.</v>

91
00:10:54.721 --> 00:10:56.782
<v Maya>Wait, so how do those two things exist at the same time?</v>

92
00:10:57.162 --> 00:11:07.047
<v Daniel>Because adoption and skill are not the same variable. Humlum proves that young workers adopt the tool more readily. But Humlum proves that older, more senior workers use it more skillfully.</v>

93
00:11:07.396 --> 00:11:15.402
<v Maya>OK, but wait, if senior people are better at it, how do we reconcile this with the famous 2025 study by Brynjolfsson, Lai and Raymond?</v>

94
00:11:16.243 --> 00:11:18.525
<v Daniel>Ah, the AI as a leveler paper.</v>

95
00:11:18.793 --> 00:11:32.722
<v Maya>Right. They looked at customer support agents and found that AI boosted the productivity of novice low-skilled workers by 15%, while the highly experienced workers barely saw any benefit at all. Do these two papers just flat out contradict each other?</v>

96
00:11:32.882 --> 00:11:47.507
<v Daniel>This is perhaps the most intellectually interesting tension in all of workplace AI research right now. But no, they don't contradict each other because they are measuring completely different environments. How so? Brynjolfsson looked at a very narrow, well-scoped tool built</v>

97
00:11:47.567 --> 00:11:54.073
<v Daniel>specifically for customer support. It literally encoded the best agent's behavior into guidance for the new agent.</v>

98
00:11:54.193 --> 00:12:09.185
<v Maya>Oh, I see. So if I can use an analogy here, the AI in the Brynjolfsson study is like giving a novice driver a GPS on a set route. It tells the new driver exactly when to turn, which makes them perform almost as well as a local who has driven the roads for 20 years. But the GenAI chat interface</v>

99
00:12:09.285 --> 00:12:18.935
<v Maya>in the Hallman study, that's an open-ended, general-purpose tool. That's not a GPS. That's dropping a high-performance Formula One engine into a chassis on an open track.</v>

100
00:12:19.195 --> 00:12:20.817
<v Daniel>Yeah, that's a perfect way to frame it.</v>

101
00:12:21.042 --> 00:12:30.304
<v Maya>A novice driver is just going to spin the tires. But an expert race car driver knows exactly how to tune it, how to push it, and how to get maximum performance out of it.</v>

102
00:12:30.564 --> 00:12:45.168
<v Daniel>It is entirely coherent that AI lifts novices the most on routine tasks with known right answers. But sophisticated use of an open-ended gen AI tool requires domain expertise. Senior people are better at delegating.</v>

103
00:12:45.589 --> 00:12:53.735
<v Daniel>They are highly practiced at specifying objectives, reviewing intermediate work, and pushing back to get exactly what they want because that is what their day job already is.</v>

104
00:12:53.835 --> 00:12:58.979
<v Maya>And they face more accountability for the final output. So they have a very real incentive to be precise.</v>

105
00:12:59.060 --> 00:12:59.560
<v Daniel>Absolutely.</v>

106
00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:06.505
<v Maya>So sophisticated AI use might just be broad professional experience wearing a new hat. It's not necessarily a separate technical skill at all.</v>

107
00:13:06.606 --> 00:13:11.910
<v Daniel>Exactly. Which explains their second big finding. Function matters just as much as seniority.</v>

108
00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:13.492
<v Maya>Meaning what department you're in.</v>

109
00:13:13.632 --> 00:13:24.405
<v Daniel>Yeah, what your department does dictates how sophisticated your AI use is. Project management, strategy, and digital innovation all score significantly above the average on sophistication.</v>

110
00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:35.962
<v Maya>Which makes intuitive sense. I mean, project managers are constantly summarizing meetings, drafting timelines, synthesizing different streams of information. Those are tasks that an open-ended tech spot is amazing</v>

111
00:13:36.062 --> 00:13:42.364
<v Daniel>at. Right. But the laggards in the data, this one hurts a bit given who provided the transcript.</v>

112
00:13:42.524 --> 00:13:43.224
<v Maya>Oh, no.</v>

113
00:13:43.644 --> 00:13:53.306
<v Daniel>Accounting and finance scored below the mean on all three sophistication measures and below the mean on all usage categories inside a major professional services and accounting firm.</v>

114
00:13:53.673 --> 00:13:58.077
<v Maya>Oh, wow. Okay, but wait, we have to provide context here. The lesson is not that accountants are bad at AI.</v>

115
00:13:58.237 --> 00:14:08.346
<v Daniel>No, no, it goes back to the workflow. A standalone text chat interface is simply a poor fit for spreadsheet-intensive, highly regulated, well-established accounting workflows.</v>

116
00:14:08.879 --> 00:14:23.643
<v Maya>Right. Accountants are all about structured data, formulas, and strict reconciliation. Trying to do complex financial math by typing paragraphs into a chatbot interface is incredibly frustrating and inefficient. The tool shape has to match the work's</v>

117
00:14:23.723 --> 00:14:24.003
<v Maya>shape.</v>

118
00:14:24.503 --> 00:14:37.867
<v Daniel>Precisely. Other research, like the Troy and Xi paper, has shown that when Gen AI is built directly into the accounting workflow, rather than existing as the separate chat window, accountants actually see massive productivity gains.</v>

119
00:14:38.114 --> 00:14:51.303
<v Maya>OK, so if the tool doesn't naturally fit certain workflows and if junior staff are struggling to drive the Formula One engine, can we just train people to use it better? You would think so. But the data on corporate training is actually the most shocking part of this paper.</v>

120
00:14:51.443 --> 00:14:54.786
<v Daniel>Yeah. This is finding number three, and it is the headline.</v>

121
00:14:54.966 --> 00:14:56.467
<v Maya>The static skill problem.</v>

122
00:14:56.527 --> 00:15:07.715
<v Daniel>It's wild. Across the entire eight-month window of the study, the authors observe no genuine trend of sophistication rising. People are using the tool, but they aren't organically getting better at it over time.</v>

123
00:15:08.104 --> 00:15:21.635
<v Maya>And what's fascinating here is how the paper addresses the corporate AI training programs. When you look at the raw data, workers who took formal AI training provided by the firm look like they were using the tool in much more sophisticated ways.</v>

124
00:15:21.775 --> 00:15:24.437
<v Daniel>Which sounds like a massive win for the HR department.</v>

125
00:15:24.617 --> 00:15:30.542
<v Maya>It does, until you look closer, because the researchers applied a statistical technique called employee fixed effects.</v>

126
00:15:31.002 --> 00:15:34.405
<v Daniel>Right. Let's translate that for a second. What does that actually mean in practice?</v>

127
00:15:34.763 --> 00:15:49.487
<v Maya>It's a statistical trick that lets researchers track one specific person over time, stripping away outside factors. So instead of just comparing Bob who took the training to Alice who didn't, they look specifically at Bob's behavior before training compared to</v>

128
00:15:49.527 --> 00:15:51.368
<v Maya>Bob's behavior after training.</v>

129
00:15:51.488 --> 00:15:58.090
<v Daniel>Oh, so it absorbs all of Bob's inherent personal traits like his natural ambition or his baseline tech savviness.</v>

130
00:15:58.330 --> 00:16:13.299
<v Maya>Exactly. And when they isolated the individuals like that, the truth emerged. Sophistication was higher only in the specific month the employee completed the training. The effect of past training is null. It just vanishes. The raw positive association</v>

131
00:16:13.319 --> 00:16:15.600
<v Maya>they initially saw was just selection bias.</v>

132
00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:22.484
<v Daniel>So highly skilled, ambitious users are just the ones choosing to sign up for the training in the first place.</v>

133
00:16:22.564 --> 00:16:28.027
<v Maya>Yes. The training isn't producing the skill. The skilled people are just seeking out the training.</v>

134
00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:39.192
<v Daniel>That is such a painful irony, because if we bring the Hunlund paper back in, in that Danish survey, workers explicitly reported that they felt held back by a perceived need for corporate training.</v>

135
00:16:39.272 --> 00:16:41.014
<v Maya>Yeah, they're practically begging for it.</v>

136
00:16:41.675 --> 00:16:56.186
<v Daniel>But the field evidence here shows that standard corporate training leaves no lasting behavioral trace. We have to state this carefully, though, because the authors are very precise. Right. They don't say the training is entirely useless. It might provide a temporary top-of-mind boost,</v>

137
00:16:56.687 --> 00:17:04.175
<v Daniel>or it might just capture employees trying out the new techniques during the week they learn them. But it does not produce a lasting change in how they interact with the AI.</v>

138
00:17:04.575 --> 00:17:18.726
<v Maya>So sending your staff to a two-hour prompting 101 seminar on a Tuesday isn't going to permanently transform them into AI whisperers. If standard training fails, how is a manager supposed to know who is actually good at this?</v>

139
00:17:19.707 --> 00:17:27.713
<v Maya>Because obviously, managers can't read 150,000 transcripts. Transcripts are expensive to process, and most firms don't even keep them for privacy reasons.</v>

140
00:17:27.988 --> 00:17:37.810
<v Daniel>Which brings us to their fourth finding, which is highly practical. The authors look for cheap metadata signals, you know, things you can see on a basic IT dashboard that actually correlate with skill.</v>

141
00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:38.771
<v Maya>Okay, what do they look at?</v>

142
00:17:38.931 --> 00:17:53.849
<v Daniel>They tested four constructs. Ambition, which is the length of the user's very first prompt. Persistence, which is how many rounds of back and forth iteration happen in a single conversation. Frequency, meaning how often they log in. And flexibility, whether they</v>

143
00:17:53.869 --> 00:17:55.991
<v Daniel>also use other tools like Microsoft Copilot.</v>

144
00:17:56.091 --> 00:17:57.231
<v Maya>And which ones actually worked?</v>

145
00:17:57.492 --> 00:18:02.414
<v Daniel>Consistently. Longer first prompts and more iteration track with more sophisticated use.</v>

146
00:18:02.494 --> 00:18:02.815
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

147
00:18:03.175 --> 00:18:05.796
<v Daniel>Frequency and multi-tool use do not reliably predict it.</v>

148
00:18:06.097 --> 00:18:21.039
<v Maya>Let's contrast that with the Dillon study on Microsoft M365 Copilot from 2025. That randomized study showed that getting people to use the tool frequently was a major precursor to seeing productivity benefits. Workers who used it</v>

149
00:18:21.099 --> 00:18:24.443
<v Maya>regularly saved about half an hour a week on email.</v>

150
00:18:25.232 --> 00:18:36.762
<v Daniel>And that highlights a crucial distinction for management. Getting people to use the tool more, which is what Dylan looked at, and getting them to use it better, which is what Hallman is measuring. Those are two completely different management problems.</v>

151
00:18:36.822 --> 00:18:37.062
<v Maya>Right.</v>

152
00:18:37.382 --> 00:18:48.011
<v Daniel>Right now, most companies only have dashboards that measure logins and seat utilization. They're measuring frequency. And this paper proves that counting logins tells you almost nothing about skill.</v>

153
00:18:48.390 --> 00:18:56.975
<v Maya>OK, I have to play devil's advocate here for a second. I need to press the limits of this paper because the skeptical case against it is strong and our listeners are smart enough to spot the holes.</v>

154
00:18:57.135 --> 00:18:57.656
<v Daniel>Bring it on.</v>

155
00:18:57.896 --> 00:19:07.282
<v Maya>First of all, it's just one firm. It's a professional services firm. Yes, back office functions like HR and marketing exist everywhere, but it's still a specific corporate culture.</v>

156
00:19:07.522 --> 00:19:09.263
<v Daniel>That is a very fair limitation.</v>

157
00:19:09.504 --> 00:19:24.060
<v Maya>Second, it's entirely descriptive, not causal. And as we discussed earlier, this sophistication measure is literally just an LLM's opinion of a human's prompt. We have zero proof that the final accounting report or marketing email actually got better or was done</v>

158
00:19:24.101 --> 00:19:24.541
<v Maya>faster.</v>

159
00:19:24.741 --> 00:19:24.982
<v Daniel>Also</v>

160
00:19:25.042 --> 00:19:39.654
<v Maya>true. But here is my biggest pushback. The window for this data closed in August 2025. In the AI world, that is ancient history. By the author's own admission, models have advanced massively since then. We have autonomous</v>

161
00:19:39.734 --> 00:19:47.040
<v Maya>agents now that act on our behalf. Does studying prompting behavior from mid-2025 tell us anything useful as we sit here in late 2026?</v>

162
00:19:47.993 --> 00:20:02.841
<v Daniel>I will absolutely hold the line on this one because the data is still unprecedented. Yes, the models have evolved, but human habits evolve much, much slower. That's true. Behaviors, cognitive approaches to delegation, and the sheer ability to articulate a clear goal, those are human skills, and they outlast any</v>

163
00:20:02.881 --> 00:20:10.306
<v Daniel>single generation of a software model. Documenting this point in time is how we establish a baseline at all. Before this, we were just guessing based on surveys.</v>

164
00:20:10.646 --> 00:20:24.556
<v Maya>So what does this all mean? The conversation around AI at work has been entirely stuck on adoption. Did we buy the tool? Did we turn it on? This deep dive looks at what happens after adoption is finished. And the answer is</v>

165
00:20:25.556 --> 00:20:26.297
<v Maya>pretty uncomfortable.</v>

166
00:20:26.357 --> 00:20:26.837
<v Daniel>Yeah, it is.</v>

167
00:20:27.037 --> 00:20:39.784
<v Maya>Skill is highly concentrated in a small group of super users. It cracks almost perfectly with the professional experience people already had. It didn't organically grow over eight months. And it stubbornly resisted standard corporate training intervention.</v>

168
00:20:39.804 --> 00:20:54.753
<v Daniel>Which forces us to really question that optimistic utopian narrative from the Brynjolfsson paper. The idea that AI is going to do this great leveler that instantly boosts the junior staff and compresses the inequality gap in the office, well, that doesn't</v>

169
00:20:54.893 --> 00:20:58.215
<v Daniel>obviously survive contact with the general purpose tool on open-ended work.</v>

170
00:20:58.435 --> 00:21:09.462
<v Maya>Right. If sophisticated AI use really is just domain expertise wearing a new costume, then these tools might actually widen the gap between the senior experts and the junior novices rather than closing it.</v>

171
00:21:09.722 --> 00:21:24.704
<v Daniel>And that raises an important, frankly chilling question to leave you with today. If driving an AI well requires deep, hard-won domain expertise, but the AI is simultaneously being used to automate away the exact entry-level junior work</v>

172
00:21:24.724 --> 00:21:29.634
<v Daniel>where that expertise used to be built, where exactly do the next generation of senior experts come from?</v>

173
00:21:30.055 --> 00:21:35.365
<v Maya>That is definitely something to mull over on your next coffee break. Thanks for joining us on this deep dive. We'll see you next time.</v>
